<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumarapala (Chaulukya dynasty)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1143–<a href="/years/1172">1172</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kumarapala was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> from about 1143 to 1172, succeeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-siddharaja">Jayasimha Siddharaja</a>. He extended the kingdom by defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamanas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> and the Paramaras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. Under the influence of the Jain scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemachandra">Hemachandra</a> he became a patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, restricting animal slaughter in his lands and building many temples, including the one at Taranga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumarapala-chaulukya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Kumarapala (Chaulukya dynasty)" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaulukyas_of_Anahillapataka_King_Kumarapala_Circa_1145-1171.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
